            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Hatshepsut's Early Life</strong><br>\nBorn in the 15th century BCE, Hatshepsut was the daughter of King Thutmose I and Queen Ahmose. From an early age, she was exposed to the affairs of state and the running of the Egyptian empire. When her father died, she was married to her half-brother Thutmose II and became queen. Her rise to power began when she was appointed regent for her stepson, Thutmose III, who was too young to rule.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Becoming Pharaoh</strong><br>\nAfter the death of her husband, Thutmose II, Hatshepsut began ruling as a regent but eventually took on the full powers and title of a pharaoh. She adopted the full regalia and symbols of a male pharaoh to assert her authority. Her reign as a pharaoh was unusual for the time, as women typically did not hold this position in the highly patriarchal society of ancient Egypt.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Reign and Accomplishments</strong><br>\nHatshepsut's reign was marked by peace and prosperity. She initiated trade expeditions, most notably to the Land of Punt, which brought back riches and resources that had been scarce. She also embarked on an ambitious building program that included her famous mortuary temple at Deir el-Bahri, which is considered one of the architectural wonders of ancient Egypt.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
