            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Economic Policies</strong><br>\nHatshepsut's economic policies greatly benefited Egypt. Her reign saw a flourishing of the arts and architecture due to the wealth accumulated from increased trade and efficient agricultural practices. She commissioned numerous construction projects, including temples and obelisks, and her attention to mining increased Egypt's wealth in gold and other precious resources.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Religious Impact</strong><br>\nHatshepsut also played a significant role in religious reform. She restored many temples and promoted the worship of Amun. Her own divine birth narrative, depicted in her mortuary temple, helped to solidify her position as pharaoh by establishing a godly right to rule. She often portrayed herself in the guise of a male pharaoh to reinforce her authority in religious matters.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Foreign Policy</strong><br>\nUnder Hatshepsut, Egypt's foreign policy was characterized by diplomacy and trade rather than military conquest. She strengthened Egypt's position by peaceful relations and through trade networks that extended Egypt's influence. This period of peace allowed the arts and economy to thrive, as resources were not diverted to military campaigns.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
