          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Public Works</strong><br>\nHatshepsut's building projects were numerous and grandiose, showcasing her power and religious devotion. Her monuments and buildings were inscribed with texts that spoke of her accomplishments and contributions to the gods, ensuring her legacy within the religious and cultural consciousness of Egypt.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hatshepsut's Image and Legacy</strong><br>\nHatshepsut faced the challenge of legitimizing her rule in a male-dominated society. She constructed her image as a traditional pharaoh, even in sculptures and paintings, depicting herself with a male body and traditional pharaoh's kilt and crown. Despite attempts by her successor to erase her from history, her legacy endures through her monumental building projects and the prosperity during her reign.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Mystery of Her Death</strong><br>\nThe details surrounding Hatshepsut's death remain unclear. She died in middle age, and the exact cause is still the subject of speculation among historians and Egyptologists. After her death, Thutmose III went on to rule Egypt for many years, and some evidence suggests he sought to diminish her legacy by defacing her monuments. However, modern discoveries have brought her story back to light, ensuring her place in history as one of Egypt's most fascinating rulers.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
